        <text x="120" y="150" font-family="Cinzel, serif" fill="#f3dd9c" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">CUBE</text>
        <text x="120" y="166" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">earth</text>
        <text x="120" y="180" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">6 · 12 · 8</text>
        <g transform="translate(200,92)" fill="none" stroke="#d7b05a" stroke-width="1"><polygon points="0,-26 22,0 0,26 -22,0"/><path d="M-22,0 L22,0 M0,-26 L0,26"/></g>
        <text x="200" y="150" font-family="Cinzel, serif" fill="#f3dd9c" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">OCTAHEDRON</text>
        <text x="200" y="166" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">air</text>
        <text x="200" y="180" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">8 · 12 · 6</text>
        <g transform="translate(280,92)" fill="none" stroke="#d7b05a" stroke-width="1"><polygon points="0,-26 25,-8 15,22 -15,22 -25,-8"/><polygon points="0,-10 12,-2 7,12 -7,12 -12,-2"/><path d="M0,-26 L0,-10 M25,-8 L12,-2 M15,22 L7,12 M-15,22 L-7,12 M-25,-8 L-12,-2"/></g>
        <text x="280" y="150" font-family="Cinzel, serif" fill="#f3dd9c" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">DODECAHEDRON</text>
        <text x="280" y="166" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">aether</text>
        <text x="280" y="180" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">12 · 30 · 20</text>
        <g transform="translate(360,92)" fill="none" stroke="#d7b05a" stroke-width="1"><polygon points="0,-26 23,-13 23,13 0,26 -23,13 -23,-13"/><path d="M0,-26 L0,26 M-23,-13 L23,13 M23,-13 L-23,13"/></g>
        <text x="360" y="150" font-family="Cinzel, serif" fill="#f3dd9c" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">ICOSAHEDRON</text>
        <text x="360" y="166" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">water</text>
        <text x="360" y="180" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">20 · 30 · 12</text>
        <text x="200" y="36" font-family="Cinzel, serif" fill="#f3dd9c" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">THE FIVE REGULAR SOLIDS</text>
        <text x="200" y="52" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">faces · edges · vertices — and there are exactly five, provably</text>
        <text x="200" y="214" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">Cube and octahedron are duals; dodecahedron and icosahedron are duals; the tetrahedron is its own.</text>
        <text x="200" y="232" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle">Kepler fitted them inside the planetary orbits in 1596. It was beautiful, and wrong.</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption><strong>Plate V — The Five Solids.</strong> There are exactly five convex polyhedra with identical regular faces — Euclid's last proposition proves it, and no amount of searching will yield a sixth. The elemental attributions are Plato's and are <em>assigned</em>, not discovered.</figcaption>
