        <text x="136" y="196" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">lens</text>
        <text x="306" y="110" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">retina (wired backwards)</text>
        <text x="326" y="200" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">optic nerve</text>
        <text x="268" y="186" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5">blind spot</text>
        <path d="M40 120 H116 M40 150 H120 M40 180 H116" stroke="#f3dd9c" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.6"/>
        <text x="200" y="262" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5">The photoreceptors face away from the light, and the wiring runs in front of them.</text>
        <text x="200" y="278" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5">Where that wiring leaves, there is a hole in your visual field. You have never seen it.</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption><strong>Plate XIII — The Eye.</strong> A famous piece of bad engineering, inherited rather than designed: the retina is installed back to front and the cable exits through the screen. Your brain paints over the resulting hole so convincingly that it took until 1660 for anyone to notice it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

