        <text x="200" y="298" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5">Two terms are known, one is guessable, and four are free parameters.</text>
        <text x="200" y="314" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" fill="#c9bda3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5">An equation with four unknowns does not estimate. It organises an argument — which is still worth doing.</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption><strong>Plate XVI — The Drake Equation.</strong> Frank Drake wrote it in 1961 as an agenda for a meeting, not a calculator. Any answer you have seen derived from it is a statement about the author's optimism, dressed as arithmetic.</figcaption>
